  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 100 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThough showers have dwindled for the time being, some energy\nwithin the northwest flow aloft will initiate another round of\nshowers/potential thunderstorms late tonight into Saturday. Pops\nstill look to peak around 60% with PW's pushing 2\", so\nlocally/efficient rain producers will be the most likely storm\nhazard, should one occur.\n\nThe strength of the broad Western U.S. ridge aloft further\nenvelops its grip eastward/across our FA during the first half \nof the week. As a result, our temps/humidity will build as well,\nwith peak headline heat indices up to around 110 degrees \npossible, particularly Monday-Tuesday. Isolated storm chances \nwithin this time period will serve only to reinforce the sticky \nhigh humidity, offering little relief. \n\nThe high retrogrades over the latter half of the week, backing \noff its peak intensity here but remaining warm with highs in the\nlower half 90s. While much of this time period looks dry, some\nmodeling suggests a more robust wave dives south and pushes a \ncold front across the Ohio Valley next weekend; expect storm\nchances then to be on the increase if this model signal holds.",
